# Task fan-out cap

One number, one rule: **at most 8 `Task` calls in flight, and at most 8 per
assistant message.**

TASK_FANOUT_INFLIGHT_CAP = 8

That identifier is pinned to
[`.cursor/hooks/lib/task-fanout-policy.mjs`](.cursor/hooks/lib/task-fanout-policy.mjs).
Raise it only in the policy constant (this file's number must match —
harness-lint checks).

## Protocol

1. **Wave, don't dump.** Launch at most 8 `Task` calls per assistant message.
   Do not put a 60-item (or 77-spec) batch in one message.
2. **Wait between waves.** Do not start the next wave until the current wave
   has Agent IDs, and prefer waiting until that wave completes. On
   `Timeout waiting for bubble creation`, treat those items as **undispatched**
   — do not retry them in the same turn.
3. **Serialize ≠ wave.** Forbidding one-at-a-time still holds; forbidding a
   60-call burst is this rule. Grilling's "never await one validator before
   dispatching the next" does **not** override this cap — waving is required.
4. **Fan-out barrier.** Every item still needs a **returned** verdict before
   `CreatePlan` / synthesis. Waving does not shrink that barrier — it only
   bounds how many Tasks are in flight at once.
5. **Timeout / orphan handling.** If the parent times out and a later
   transcript still arrives, do not double-dispatch that item; the first
   returned brief wins.

## Who waves

The **parent orchestrator** waves at the cap. Each `feedback-validator` /
`spec-verifier` still owns exactly one item. `/sdd-to-tdd` stays one Task per
phase — no behavior change.

## Mechanical backstop

A fail-open `preToolUse` hook (`task-fanout-guard.mjs`, matcher `Task`) denies
a `Task` call when reservations are at the cap and tells the agent to wave
and wait for in-flight to drop. A hook crash must not brick TDD `Task` calls.
